}
react-markdown
does not allow raw HTML in Markdown by default. If you enable the rehype-raw
plugin to support embedded HTML, you are back in XSS territory and need sanitization again. Without rehype-raw
, react-markdown
is inherently safe.
rehype-react
For AST-based rendering, rehype-react
converts a rehype (HTML AST) tree into React elements. This is useful when you have a processing pipeline that transforms HTML through multiple plugins:
npm install rehype-react unified rehype-parse
const processor = unified()
.use(rehypeParse, { fragment: true })
.use(rehypeSanitize) // Sanitization built into the pipeline
.use(rehypeReact, {
createElement,
Fragment,
components: {
a: (props: any) => <a {...props} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" />,
},
});
function ProcessedHTML({ html }: { html: string }) {
const result = processor.processSync(html).result;
return <div>{result}</div>;
}
The advantage of rehype-react
is that sanitization is a pipeline step via rehype-sanitize
, and you get the full power of the unified ecosystem for transforming content.
Comparison: when to use which
| Approach | Best for | Sanitization | Virtual DOM | Perf overhead |
|---|---|---|---|---|
dangerouslySetInnerHTML + DOMPurify |
CMS HTML, legacy content, performance-critical rendering | Manual (DOMPurify) | No (bypasses it) | Lowest |
html-react-parser |
HTML that needs React component integration, interactive elements | Manual (DOMPurify) | Yes | Medium |
react-markdown |
Markdown source content | Built-in (no raw HTML by default) | Yes | Medium |
rehype-react |
Processing pipelines, plugin-based transformation | Built-in (rehype-sanitize ) |
Yes | Highest |
For most applications rendering CMS content, dangerouslySetInnerHTML
with DOMPurify is the most straightforward and performant approach. Use html-react-parser
when you need to replace specific elements with React components. Use react-markdown
when your source is Markdown. Use rehype-react
when you need a full AST processing pipeline.
Next.js considerations
Next.js adds complexity because components can render on the server, the client, or both. Here is how to handle HTML rendering in a Next.js application.
Server Components and HTML rendering
In Next.js App Router, Server Components cannot use hooks like useMemo
or browser APIs. If you are rendering CMS content in a Server Component, sanitize during data fetching:
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx (Server Component)
async function getPost(slug: string) {
const res = await fetch(`https://cms.example.com/api/posts/${slug}`);
const post = await res.json();
return {
...post,
// Sanitize on the server during data fetching
body: DOMPurify.sanitize(post.body, {
ALLOWED_TAGS: ['p', 'h1', 'h2', 'h3', 'strong', 'em', 'a', 'img', 'ul', 'ol', 'li',
'blockquote', 'pre', 'code', 'br', 'hr', 'table', 'thead', 'tbody', 'tr', 'th', 'td'],
ALLOWED_ATTR: ['href', 'src', 'alt', 'class', 'target', 'rel', 'loading'],
}),
};
}
export default async function BlogPost({
params,
}: {
params: Promise<{ slug: string }>;
}) {
const { slug } = await params;
const post = await getPost(slug);
return (
<article>
<h1>{post.title}</h1>
<div
className="prose"
dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: post.body }}
/>
</article>
);
}
This is the ideal pattern for Next.js. Sanitization happens on the server during data fetching. The sanitized HTML is serialized as part of the Server Component output. No sanitization library needs to be shipped to the client. The client receives pre-sanitized HTML.
Sanitizing in API routes and data fetching
For getServerSideProps
(Pages Router) or API routes, sanitize before passing data to the page:
// pages/blog/[slug].tsx (Pages Router)
export const getServerSideProps: GetServerSideProps = async ({ params }) => {
const res = await fetch(`https://cms.example.com/api/posts/${params?.slug}`);
const post = await res.json();
return {
props: {
...post,
body: DOMPurify.sanitize(post.body),
},
};
};
Sanitizing on the server has two benefits: you avoid shipping DOMPurify to the client (saving approximately 60KB from your bundle), and you ensure the HTML is clean before it ever reaches the browser.
Client-side sanitization when needed
If you need to sanitize on the client - for example, in a live preview where the user is typing HTML content - use the standard dompurify
package in a Client Component:
'use client';
export function HTMLPreview() {
const [html, setHtml] = useState('');
const sanitized = useMemo(() => DOMPurify.sanitize(html), [html]);
return (
<div className="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4">
<textarea
value={html}
onChange={(e) => setHtml(e.target.value)}
className="font-mono p-2 border rounded"
rows={20}
/>
<div
className="prose p-2 border rounded"
dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: sanitized }}
/>
</div>
);
}
Testing for XSS vulnerabilities
Shipping a sanitizer is not enough. You need to verify it works. Here is how to test your HTML rendering for XSS vulnerabilities.
Automated XSS testing with payloads
Create a test suite that throws known XSS payloads at your sanitization:
const XSS_PAYLOADS = [
// Script injection
'<script>alert("XSS")</script>',
'<script/src="https://evil.com/xss.js"></script>',
// Event handler injection
'<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>',
'<svg onload=alert(1)>',
'<body onload=alert(1)>',
'<input onfocus=alert(1) autofocus>',
'<marquee onstart=alert(1)>',
'<div onmouseover=alert(1)>hover me</div>',
'<details open ontoggle=alert(1)>',
'<video><source onerror=alert(1)>',
// javascript: protocol
'<a href="javascript:alert(1)">click</a>',
'<a href="JAVASCRIPT:alert(1)">click</a>',
'<a href="java\nscript:alert(1)">click</a>',
'<a href="javascript:alert(1)">click</a>',
'<iframe src="javascript:alert(1)">',
// data: URI
'<a href="data:text/html,<script>alert(1)</script>">click</a>',
'<object data="data:text/html,<script>alert(1)</script>">',
// SVG-based
'<svg><script>alert(1)</script></svg>',
'<svg><animate onbegin=alert(1) attributeName=x dur=1s>',
'<svg><set onbegin=alert(1) attributename=x to=1>',
// CSS-based
'<div style="background:url(javascript:alert(1))">',
'<div style="behavior:url(xss.htc)">',
// Mutation XSS
'<noscript><p title="</noscript><img src=x onerror=alert(1)>">',
'<math><mtext><table><mglyph><style><!--</style><img src=x onerror=alert(1)>',
// Encoding tricks
'<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>',
'<img src=x onerror=\\u0061lert(1)>',
];
describe('XSS prevention', () => {
XSS_PAYLOADS.forEach((payload, index) => {
it(`blocks XSS payload #${index + 1}`, () => {
const { container } = render();
const html = container.innerHTML;
// No script tags should survive
expect(html).not.toMatch(/<script/i);
// No event handlers should survive
expect(html).not.toMatch(/on\w+=/i);
// No javascript: URIs should survive
expect(html).not.toMatch(/javascript:/i);
// No data: URIs in hrefs should survive
expect(html).not.toMatch(/href\s*=\s*["']data:/i);
});
});
});
ESLint rules
The eslint-plugin-react
package includes a react/no-danger
rule that flags all uses of dangerouslySetInnerHTML
:
npm install --save-dev eslint-plugin-react
{
"rules": {
"react/no-danger": "warn"
}
}
Setting this to warn
rather than error
is pragmatic. You want developers to notice it and justify it, but you do not want to ban it entirely since there are legitimate use cases. In code review, every dangerouslySetInnerHTML
should have a comment explaining why it is needed and confirming that the input is sanitized.
For a stricter approach, you can use error
and require explicit ESLint disable comments with justification:
// eslint-disable-next-line react/no-danger -- sanitized with DOMPurify in getPost()
<div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: post.sanitizedBody }} />
Code review checklist
When you encounter dangerouslySetInnerHTML
in a pull request, verify these items:
-
Is the HTML sanitized? Look for
DOMPurify.sanitize()
or equivalent. If not, reject the PR. - Where does the HTML come from? Trace the data source. User input is highest risk. CMS content from trusted authors is lower risk but still needs sanitization.
- Is sanitization happening at the right layer? Server-side sanitization is preferred over client-side for SSR applications.
-
Is the DOMPurify config appropriate? An overly permissive config (allowing
style
tags,form
tags, ordata-*
attributes) expands the attack surface. - Is there a CSP in place? Defense-in-depth matters.
- Are there tests for XSS payloads? The test suite should include known XSS vectors.
Best practices summary
After spending years working with HTML rendering in React applications, these are the practices I follow on every project.
Always sanitize with DOMPurify. No exceptions. Even if the HTML comes from "your own" API. Even if the content was written by "trusted" authors. APIs get compromised. Databases get injected into. Admins make mistakes. Sanitize everything.
Use CSP as defense-in-depth. CSP is not a replacement for sanitization, but it is a critical second layer. If sanitization fails for any reason, CSP prevents the injected code from executing. Deploy it in report-only mode first, then enforce.
Prefer structured data over raw HTML. If you control the content authoring experience, consider storing content as structured JSON (like Portable Text in Sanity, or a custom schema) instead of HTML strings. This eliminates the HTML rendering problem entirely because you render known-safe components from structured data.
Sanitize on output, not input. A common mistake is sanitizing HTML when it is submitted and storing the clean version. This seems reasonable but has two problems: your sanitization rules may change (new attack vectors are discovered), and you lose the original content. Sanitize when you render instead. Store the original, sanitize on display.
Audit regularly. DOMPurify handles known bypass techniques, but new mutation XSS vectors are discovered periodically. Keep DOMPurify updated. Run your XSS payload test suite in CI. Subscribe to security advisories.
Never trust user input. This is the fundamental principle. Not from the frontend. Not from the API. Not from the database. Not from a CMS. Not from a "trusted" third-party service. Sanitize everything that gets rendered as HTML.
Common mistakes that lead to XSS
I want to close with the mistakes I see most often in production codebases. These are not hypothetical - I have seen every one of these in real code reviews.
Trusting "internal" API data. The reasoning goes: "This HTML comes from our own backend, not from users, so it is safe." This ignores the reality that backends have vulnerabilities too. SQL injection into a content table means the attacker controls your "internal" HTML. A compromised admin account means an attacker can inject malicious content through your CMS. An API that aggregates data from third parties means you are trusting their sanitization. Never trust the source. Sanitize the output.
Sanitizing on input instead of output. If you sanitize HTML when it is submitted and store the sanitized version, you are locking in today's sanitization rules. When a new bypass technique is discovered next month, all of your stored "clean" HTML is now potentially vulnerable. You have no way to re-sanitize it because you discarded the originals. Always store the original content and sanitize at render time.
Using regex to strip HTML tags. This is one of the most dangerous anti-patterns in web security:
// NEVER DO THIS - regex cannot safely parse HTML
function stripTags(html: string): string {
return html.replace(/<[^>]*>/g, '');
}
// This trivially bypasses it:
// <img src=x onerror=alert(1)// (missing closing bracket)
// <scr<script>ipt>alert(1)</script>
// <<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>
HTML is not a regular language. Regular expressions cannot parse it correctly. There are hundreds of ways to construct valid HTML that bypasses regex-based stripping. Use a proper DOM-based parser like DOMPurify that actually builds and walks the DOM tree.
Forgetting about attribute-based XSS. Many developers focus on stripping <script>
tags and consider themselves safe. But as I showed earlier, the most common XSS vectors in the context of innerHTML
are attribute-based: onerror
, onload
, onmouseover
, onfocus
, href="javascript:..."
, and dozens of other event handler attributes. A sanitizer that only strips <script>
tags is almost useless.
Not sanitizing Markdown output. Developers sometimes assume that because they control the Markdown-to-HTML conversion, the output is safe. But if the Markdown source comes from user input and the parser supports raw HTML in Markdown (most do by default), the user can embed arbitrary HTML in their Markdown content. Either disable raw HTML in your Markdown parser or sanitize the output.
Using dangerouslySetInnerHTML
for content you control. If you are rendering static content that you wrote and is hardcoded in your codebase, you do not need dangerouslySetInnerHTML
. Use JSX. The whole point of React is that JSX gives you a safe, composable way to build HTML. Reserve dangerouslySetInnerHTML
for cases where the HTML genuinely comes from outside your React component tree.
The name dangerouslySetInnerHTML
exists because the React team wanted you to think twice before using it. Take the hint. When you do use it, sanitize with DOMPurify, deploy CSP, test with XSS payloads, and audit your code regularly. The difference between a secure application and a data breach is often a single missing sanitization call.
